s also  being debated among eu but also  nato member states  there is concern that germany could  become too dependent on russian gas  a concern we were able to defuse but  it's also about the concerns and the  legitimate interests of ukraine and  poland  which have to be negotiated into the  contracts  and when it comes to the u.s threat of  sanctions we in the german government  have the clear position that these are  not in accordance with international law  and michaela who conducted that  interview joins us now from our politics  studio michaela  underground karan bauer isn't just  germany's defense minister she's also  the chair  of chancellor angela merkel's cdu party  and of course a close ally  of the chancellor what else did she have  to say about the north stream pipeline  controversy  well you can sense that there is a high  level of concern over what the united  states may do  next at the same time i also know from  previous conversations that she never  was a big fan of this particular project  in fact there were german mps  european mps who signed up against it  saying that it weakened  europe's integrity and sovereignty  particularly towards russia  and with the u.s of course applying  pressure and having its very own  interests of selling um  liquefied gas to the european union so  for now the german  government is trying to build a bridge  by  building also a new port for gas that  could be imported from the united states  but clearly there is a deadlock here and  referring to international law that's  the big questions how far  that argument will get the german  government which after all  continues to say that the u.s are their  closest ally  and speaking of the u.s the trump  administration has taken  a very hard line on this pipeline but  with uh presidential elections due in  four months time  do you think berlin is thinking of just  sitting out this confrontation for now  well of course berlin has also heard all  the politicians hear that joe